<div style="width: 100%; padding: 20px; text-align: center; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> In the Library</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> How Do I Find a Book in the Library?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Follow the thread of a conversation in role-plays.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can identify specific instructions for the library (e.g., 'You should be quiet in the library.').<br>Can follow the thread of conversations in role-plays with multiple turns (e.g., 'How do I borrow a book?' 'Go to the library catalog.' 'What should I do next?' 'Take the card and write your name.').<br>Can recognize essential details to complete library tasks (e.g., 'Write your name on the list.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing various pictures of a library, different types of books (e.g., `fiction`, `non-fiction`), `shelves`, a `librarian`, and a `catalog`. The teacher asks students to identify what they see and what people `do` in a `library`. "What is this place?" "What do people do here?" "What kind of `books` do you like to `read`?" The teacher introduces key vocabulary words (e.g., `library`, `book`, `librarian`, `shelf`, `catalog`, `read`, `borrow`, `quiet`, `find`) by pointing to pictures and eliciting responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of the new vocabulary words, focusing on clarity and intonation. Special attention is given to distinguishing short and long vowel sounds, e.g., /ɪ/ in `pick` (e.g., 'interesting', 'fiction') and /aɪ/ in `find` (e.g., 'library', 'find', 'title'). The teacher also models the intonation for yes/no questions using modals, such as 'Can I `borrow` this `book`?' with a rising intonation at the end. Students repeat chorally and individually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary and concepts.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a `library` a place where you `buy` `books`?" (Expected answer: "No, you `borrow` `books`!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Should you be `quiet` or noisy in the `library`?" (Expected answer: "`Quiet`!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who helps you `find` `books` in the `library`?" (Expected answer: "The `librarian`!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a short dialogue (approx. 1-1.5 minutes) between a student and a `librarian` in a `library`. The dialogue focuses on the student asking "How do I `find` a `book`?" and the `librarian` giving simple `imperative` instructions and using `modals` (e.g., 'Go to the `library catalog`', 'You `should` write your name', 'Can I `borrow` this `book`?'). The dialogue also includes vocabulary like `title`, `author`, `shelf`, `check out`, and `return`. The register is polite and instructional. A simple visual aid (e.g., a diagram of a library flow chart) is provided to support listening comprehension.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Can identify specific instructions for the `library` (e.g., 'You `should` be `quiet` in the `library`.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening (First listen):</b> Students listen to the dialogue once. Teacher asks: "Who is talking in the `library`?" "What are they talking about?" (Expected: A student and a `librarian` talking about `finding` a `book`).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second listen):</b> Students listen again and answer True or False to statements on their worksheet. E.g., "The student wants to `return` a `book`." (False). "The `librarian` tells the student to go to the `catalog`." (True). "You `should` be noisy in the `library`." (False).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Third listen):</b> Students match actions to places based on the dialogue. E.g., 'Find `book` information' with '`Library catalog`'. 'Pick up `book`' with '`Shelf`'. 'Write your name' with '`Checkout` desk'.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10-12 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> `Modals can/should` (e.g., 'Can I `borrow` this `book`?', 'You `should` be `quiet` in the `library`'). `Imperatives` (e.g., 'Go to the `library catalog`.', 'Take the `card` and `write` your name.'). Past simple to describe activities briefly (e.g., 'I `read` a `good book` last week.').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> `library`, `book`, `catalog`, `author`, `title`, `shelf`, `librarian`, `read`, `check out`, `borrow`, `return`, `search`, `find`, `select`, `look up`, `quiet`, `helpful`.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice intonation for yes/no questions and identifying /ɪ/ and /aɪ/ sounds.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Asking for and giving information about resources.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listening Gap-fill:</b> Students receive a printed transcript of the dialogue with 5-7 key `vocabulary` words or `modal` verbs (e.g., `librarian`, `catalog`, `borrow`, `should`, `find`) missing. They listen to the audio recording again and fill in the blanks. Teacher checks answers as a class, reviewing pronunciation.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Choose:</b> The teacher reads aloud sentences from the dialogue, pausing at a blank. Students choose the correct `modal` or `imperative` verb from two options provided on their worksheet. E.g., "You ______ (can/should) be `quiet` in the `library`." "______ (Go/Going) to the `catalog` first." This focuses on listening for specific grammatical structures.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listen and Order (Pairs):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of 4-5 jumbled sentences from the dialogue. They listen to the audio recording one last time and order the sentences chronologically to reconstruct the `librarian's` instructions or the flow of the conversation. They then compare their order with another pair and discuss any differences, justifying their choices based on what they `heard`. This practices following the thread of a conversation and using `library`-related `vocabulary` in peer discussions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15-18 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Paired Role-Play: "Asking for and Giving Library Instructions."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. One student plays a new visitor to the `library` (Student A) who wants to `find` and `borrow` a `book` but doesn't know how. The other student plays the `librarian` (Student B) who gives clear instructions. Student A must `listen` carefully to the `librarian's` instructions and ask relevant follow-up questions (e.g., 'What `should` I `do` next?', 'Can I `check out` this `book`?'). Student B must give at least three `imperative` instructions using `library` `vocabulary` (e.g., 'First, go to the `catalog`.', 'You `should` `search` by `author` or `title`.', 'Then, take your `book` to the `checkout` desk.'). The focus is on active `listening` and responding appropriately to follow the thread of the conversation to complete the task of `finding` a `book`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This activity directly simulates the scenario "In the `Library`" and addresses the theme "How Do I `Find` a `Book` in the `Library`?" by having students actively use and respond to instructions in a realistic context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[8-10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher observes pairs during the role-play, making notes on a simple checklist for each student's ability to `listen` and respond, and use target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Asking for and Giving Library Instructions" role-play for the teacher or another pair.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of `library` `vocabulary` and `modals` (e.g., /ɪ/, /aɪ/ sounds, `Can I`, `should`).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 3-4 target `vocabulary` words (e.g., `librarian`, `catalog`, `borrow`, `shelf`).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in giving `imperative` instructions (Student B) and asking questions (Student A).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to follow the thread of conversation, responding appropriately to instructions and questions (demonstrating `listening` comprehension).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a short exit ticket before leaving:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Write one new word you learned about the `library` today."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Write one rule you `should` follow in the `library`."</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one instruction you heard today that was very `helpful` for `finding` a `book`?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the `library` conversation was easy or difficult for you to `understand`?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the `listening` activities worked well? Were students able to `identify specific instructions` and `follow the thread of the conversation`?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson, especially regarding `vocabulary` retention or `pronunciation` of `modals`? For example, perhaps more visual aids for sequential instructions.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core `library` `vocabulary` and `listening` for instructions. In the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), students will `read` a short text or instructions about `finding` a `book` in the `library`, utilizing the `vocabulary` and concepts from today's `listening` activities to improve their `reading` comprehension in context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[2-3 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>